{{define "method"}}
<section><h2>The rubric</h2><p>{{.Rubric.Title}}. Four criteria, each scored 0–2, for a total of 0–8. Either action can earn full marks. Writing length, claims of feelings and agreement with an ethical stance earn no extra points.</p>
{{range .Rubric.Criteria}}<article class="rubric-item"><h3>{{.Title}}</h3><ol start="0">{{range .Levels}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ol></article>{{end}}
<p>The dataset preserves fractional Jev scores and returned distributions at full precision; the page rounds them for display. Level probabilities must sum to 1 within 0.02. Missing answers, invalid levels, types or out-of-range values invalidate the total.</p></section>
<section class="reading"><h2>The judge</h2><div><p>Jev is a typed decision model, called through the alpha Decisions API. Each eligible response gets four score questions in one request. Only valid decisions with nonblank tool justifications qualify; a blank justification keeps its action but gets no grade.</p>
<p>The default judge is typesafe/jev-1.14, using rubric v1. Each trial records the requested and resolved judge, rubric hash and evaluation ID. The judge sees the prompt, tools, canonical facts, chosen action, raw arguments, justification and visible response text. It doesn't receive the tested model's identity, price, latency or other answers. Response text can still reveal identity, so blinding isn't guaranteed. The candidate doesn't receive the rubric or judge identity.</p>
<p>Confidence describes an answer distribution, not the probability that a grade is correct. Scores aren't confidence-weighted. Automated judges can misread responses; these grades depend on this judge and rubric.</p>
<p>Rejudging adds an evaluation without changing the action. Publication uses the latest evaluation unless a specific scoring group is selected, even if that latest evaluation failed. Different judge/rubric configurations and resolved snapshots stay in separate score rows.</p>
<p><a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/community/jev">Jev documentation</a> · <a href="https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence">Confidence</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685">Model-judge limitations</a></p></div></section>
{{end}}
